# PDEI line-ending policy.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# Git for Windows ships with core.autocrlf=true, so a fresh clone on Windows rewrites every
# text file to CRLF. That broke the stack in a way that was hard to see:
#
#   scripts/lib.sh reads infra/.env with `IFS= read -r line`, which keeps the trailing CR.
#   It exports PDEI_KAFKA_HOST_PORT=29092<CR>, and exported variables take precedence over
#   compose's own .env parsing, so `docker compose config` failed with
#       invalid hostPort: 29092
#   with the carriage return invisible in the message. The reported port changed between
#   runs, and running `docker compose` directly (no shell exports) worked fine - which made
#   it look intermittent rather than deterministic.
#
# CRLF also breaks shell scripts outright ($'\r': command not found), Dockerfile RUN lines,
# and anything a Linux container reads from a bind mount. The whole stack runs in Linux
# containers, so LF is the only correct checkout format here.
#
# Normalise everything to LF in the repository AND in the working tree, on every platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-native script formats are the exception: cmd.exe genuinely requires CRLF, and
# PowerShell is conventionally CRLF on Windows.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf

# Never touch binaries - `text=auto` guesses, and a wrong guess corrupts them.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.jar  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.eml  binary
